# Development environment and verification

Required workspace procedure, selected by the routing table in `AGENTS.md`.
Its rules retain workspace scope and precedence. Paths and commands are relative
to the coordination workspace unless the text specifies another repository.

For queued merges/deployments, complete builds, tests and review before submitting
the prepared snapshot. Resource contention is a wait, not a failed check. Follow
[coordination](../coordination.md) for interruptible waiting and stale-snapshot
refresh; repeat only the checks/review required by the changed patch or baseline.

## Development Environment

Use the generic `dev-session-monitor` skill for authorized tests, CI checks and
builds expected to exceed one minute, and delegate uncertain-duration integration
tests and builds before launching them. Resolve the fresh verification watcher
from the installed catalog's separate utility policy. It is not a team member
and must not edit source, diagnose, retry, approve, or deploy.
Keep known quick checks inline. Resolve planning, implementation and diagnosis
settings from the retained team roster. Mandatory review uses an eligible
retained review-purpose member's saved model and effort; without one, use a
review-purpose role from the installed catalog's default development team in a
fresh standalone thread, including for solo sessions. Do not invent a team or
change the roster. Run final review only after the substantive deliverable is
complete, all intended changes are committed, and quick checks pass, before
long integration tests. Completed substantive documentation and configuration
deliverables remain in scope. Routine planning, investigation, findings,
session tracking and evidence alone never trigger automatic review. Earlier
review requires an explicit user request, is advisory, and does not replace
final review. A reviewer in a preset receives no automatic assignment. Pass
project escalation rules, including
unexpected local kernel builds, in the watcher's brief. The parent continues
automatically on completion or escalation. If the skill or delegation is
unavailable, report
that once and use minimal-output parent monitoring. A user's instruction not to
wait for CI takes precedence.

Development is generally Nix-based. Prefer each repository's `nix develop`,
`nix-shell`, flake outputs, or documented development shell before running
language-specific tools. Deployment is usually to NixOS or vpsAdminOS systems,
often through `confctl` and the configuration repositories.

Treat an unexpected local Linux kernel build in vpsAdminOS development or test
workflows as a bug unless the current work intentionally changes kernel sources
or configuration. vpsAdminOS kernels should normally be substituted from the
vpsAdminOS binary cache after GitHub Actions or its runners build them. When a
command starts building a kernel, stop it and investigate why the derivation
missed the cache. A local rebuild is acceptable only when the kernel or its
configuration is intentionally changed, or when the responsible runner has not
yet built and published the expected derivation; record the justification in
the initiative `state.md`.
If the work needs an additional kernel output, update the vpsAdminOS CI builder
to build and publish that output as part of the same initiative instead of
relying on recurring local builds.

When running `vpsadmin-devcluster`, use the bridge network by
default. Do not choose `--network local` unless the user explicitly asks for it
or the bridge network is genuinely unavailable; if local networking is used,
record the reason in the initiative state.

Use GitHub Actions as a feedback loop after pushing branches. If `gh` is not
available in the current shell, run it through Nix, for example
`nix shell nixpkgs#gh -c gh run list ...`. Inspect failed logs, monitor reruns,
and resolve failures instead of leaving CI for the user to chase.
Successful checks, review, and CI are verification evidence, not permission to
merge or push feature content to a default branch; follow the Git approval gate.
Before calling an unmerged feature branch ready, give the independent reviewer
its complete cleaned base-to-head commit series, final diff, and migration
provenance under the mandatory-change-review workflow. Require explicit
conclusions on obsolete history and migration lineage, including "no migrations"
when applicable.
Record findings and remaining deployment limits in the initiative state.

After a force-push or a follow-up fix push, cancel superseded queued or
in-progress GitHub Actions workflow runs for the same branch. Only cancel runs
whose `headSha` no longer matches the current branch head; do not cancel
workflows for other branches or workflows already running on the current head.

When creating or editing GitHub workflows, verify the latest upstream version of
each imported action from its official repository before choosing the `uses:`
ref. Do not rely on remembered version numbers; use the newest compatible
version unless the workflow records a specific reason to pin an older one.

Rerunning a failed GitHub Actions job is not a substitute for investigation.
Before accepting a rerun as validation, download or open the failed attempt's
logs and artifacts, identify the root cause as far as the available evidence
allows, and record the finding in the initiative `state.md`. If the artifacts
are insufficient, improve the test or runner diagnostics rather than treating a
green rerun as proof that the failure did not matter. Prefer fixing the
underlying problem; when the failure is unrelated to the current change, record
the evidence for that conclusion.

When a repository is missing a tool in the ambient shell, enter the repository's
Nix shell or use an appropriate `nix shell` command. Do not work around missing
tooling by recording local environment limitations in commit messages.

When adding new integration tests that use the vpsAdminOS test-runner, write
test scripts in the current RSpec-style structure with examples and
expectations, such as `describe`, `it`, and `expect`. Do not refactor existing
tests solely to convert their style unless the user explicitly asks for that
refactor.
# Threadless installation integration, 2026-10-13

This dated, source-only route completes the coordination installation owned by
`2026-10-13-workspace-coordination`. It is not installed, exported through Nix,
exposed in the portal or used by automatic workers. Ordinary bootstrap selectors
still cannot prepare, approve or submit new work. Managed sessions use the normal
[coordinator](../coordination.md).

The initiating user authorized the four repositories' master integrations after
the session-creation fix was deployed. The helper binds that condition to frozen
successful deployment evidence. It uses the same retained threadless owner and
reservation. Each operation integrates one reviewed repository, head and target.
It creates no conversation, transfers no ownership and never deploys or releases
a machine. The selected runtime owns snapshots, receipts, claims, workers,
fast-forward Git integration, hooks and independent reconciliation.

## Prerequisites

Read the selected runtime's complete coordination and recovery guides with
`workspace-host coordination guide`. Read the Git, lifecycle and verification
procedures and complete ordinary final history/review/verification gates.
Capture comparisons before each integration; retain feature refs.

Finish the creation-fix deployment first. Its driver units and all owned jobs
must have settled successfully. Keep the same reservation in deployed_review.
Freeze a plan beneath the owned tracking directory containing schema 1, workspace,
workspaceName, slug, ownerId, reservationId, selectedPackage, profileToken,
catalogDigest, repositories (names mapped to exact head and full target), evidence,
deploymentEvidence and source. Source records the exact conditional user direction;
deploymentEvidence is a checksummed successful live-check result also included in
evidence. Include independent final review and exact verification evidence.
Workspace final head may descend from the deployed source when source-only code
leaves its application derivation identical; retain that equivalence evidence.

The helper accepts only this installation's owner, workspace, four repositories
and master targets. Run with the selected runtime's Ruby and declared Nix
environment. It loads the raw installed workspace-host from the selected profile,
not worktree runtime code. No state/profile/registry override or fixture mode.
Freeze and review the helper commit and application-derivation identity before
use. Exact plan and evidence hashes must remain unchanged for retries.

The lead holds a supervised maintenance window: start no other bootstrap deploy
or merge driver for this owner until serial integration finishes. A process lock
serializes this helper, and ordinary admission verifies there is no other pending
owner job. The process lock does not exclude the separate deployment bootstrap.
This route relies on the trusted lead's exclusion; it does not promise concurrent
bootstrap submission support. Other sessions keep their normal queue/resources.
If that exclusion cannot be guaranteed, stop and add a reviewed exact-job claim
interface to the owning runtime instead.

## Prepare, execute and retry

Use a fresh lowercase UUID P for a preparation request:

```json
{"schema":1,"requestId":"UUID-P","repository":"dev-workspace"}
```

Run the reviewed helper by its absolute path in the runtime repository's Nix
environment, retaining stdout and stderr separately. HELPER below is
`worktrees/2026-10-13-workspace-coordination/workspace/bin/bootstrap-merge-2026-10-13`
under the absolute workspace root:

```sh
ruby HELPER --plan PLAN --request PREPARATION --prepare
```

Preparation builds hooks and retains the target checkout outside admission locks.
Its result is the execution request with schema, a fixed UUID E and immutable
artifact ID. Save it exactly, then run:

```sh
ruby HELPER --plan PLAN --request EXECUTION
```

Use a parent-launched retained service for execution and a fresh verification
watcher to observe the wait. The driver reports the retained job ID before waiting.
Exit 0 with state succeeded identifies the completed merge and its remote proof.
Do this serially for runtime, extension, configuration and workspace. Register the
results in the initiating rollout/state. Each repository integrates separately.

Exact preparation retries return the retained bundle before rebuilding or making
another target checkout when no owned work is pending. Once its merge is pending,
retry the saved execution request E; preparation admission reports busy until
owned work settles. Execution retries reuse ordinary approval and submission
receipts, even after the target checkout has changed through successful integration.
An altered request with the same UUID refuses. Do not edit bundles or create new
IDs to avoid a pending claim. All new admission and claims recheck the selected
generation, active incarnation, lifecycle journals, reservation and supervised
owner exclusion. Hook preparation and worker/recovery waits hold no admission lock.

An interrupted driver does not stop accepted workers. Retry the exact execution
request: it consumes existing receipts and starts only the original independent
reconciliation service. Unknown effects retain the claim. A claim without durable
request.json retains the installed guide's operator-recovery requirement; this
helper never redispatches it. A package/generation change stops new admission;
ordinary retained-bootstrap reconciliation remains the recovery interface.

There is no refresh action. For an unclaimed needs_refresh result, preserve its
old evidence, cancel through the ordinary exact-owner command with current revision
and a fresh cancellation ID, then rebase/review/verify and prepare a new P.
Never cancel or replace an ambiguous claimed attempt to escape recovery.

## Reviewed rebases after another integration

An exact patch-equivalent rebase can preserve the existing repository/target
approval under the Git procedure. Keep the deployed predecessor ref, complete
old/new ranges, ordered patch IDs, canonical range-diff and independent review.
Material replacements remain outside this dated route.

An optional plan `lineageEvidence` list contains review references also present
in `evidence`. Each bounded schema-1 receipt has `ownerId`, `reservationId`,
`mapping`, `decision: "accepted"`, the independent `reviewer` and `artifacts`.
Mapping fields are `repository`, full `target`, `from`, `fromBase`, `to`,
`toBase` and `relation: "patch_equivalent"`. Artifacts contain exactly one
`range_diff`, `verification` and `migration_review` reference, all included in
the frozen plan evidence. This is a pre-approval receipt; it has no approval ID.

The helper checks applied owned deployment coverage, exact deployed/final heads,
nonempty nonancestor ranges, the selected runtime's ordered patch comparator
and canonical range-diff. Duplicate, unused or changed mappings refuse. The
prepared operation must use `toBase` as both comparison and expected remote base.
Completed retries check those immutable fields without requiring today's remote
head to remain at that old base.

Freeze a new remaining-merges plan and use fresh preparation IDs. Preserve plans
and requests already admitted; an old bundle cannot be paired with a new plan.
Do not revise old evidence to escape a stale or uncertain operation.

After successful submission, obtain the actual approval record ID from the job's
`approvalId`. The bundle's `approvalId` is only the approve request UUID. Verify
the actual record's owner, merge scope, user source and retained snapshot binds
`to`, `toBase` and target. Create a separate canonical finish review receipt with
that ID added to the reviewed mapping and the same independent reviewer and
artifacts. Its outer replacement entry adds the ordinary review evidence ref.
Keep the pre-approval receipt unchanged. The independent review covers this
deterministic post-submission binding; preparing it does not authorize finish.

## Compatibility and finish

This route uses the existing runtime schemas and worker envelope without
changing dependency pins. The
helper stores ordinary merge approvals/jobs, opaque bounded preparation receipts
and immutable artifacts through the selected engine. Existing readers can load
and reconcile them. Existing automatic workers still skip new bootstrap claims.
The application derivation must be identical before omitting another deployment.

Successful merges do not finish deployed review or release aitherdev. The original
reservation and deployment history remain held. Normal finish still requires exact
final/deployed integration proofs, settled operations and explicit user direction
that deployment/review is finished. Archive, delete, reboot and exceptional release
are outside this route.

